in this video you're gonna learn how to  find the remaining factors of a  polynomial when you're given one of the  factors so let's dive into this problem  we've got f of x equals x cubed plus  five x squared plus two X minus eight  and they're telling us that X plus two  is a factor what does that mean  X plus two is a factor it means that  this will divide in evenly into this  polynomial without any remainder now  when the factor divides into a  polynomial but not any remainder that  means that if you were to set this equal  to zero  okay X plus 2 equal to 0 you get x  equals negative 2 negative 2 is a zero  what that means is it's gonna cross the  x-axis when you graph this polynomial at  negative 2 now when you do the synthetic  division if your factor is x plus 2 well  we're here you're gonna put negative 2  if it was X minus 2 then you would put  positive 2 so it's like the opposite  sign or you can think of it as you know  putting a zero in here so now when we do  this synthetic division we're going to  use the coefficients 1 X cubed 5 x  squared 2x and negative 8 keep in mind  if you're missing any of the terms like  if I went from X cubed 2x to the first  and I skipped over x squared I have to  have to put a zero there as a  placeholder so you want to go in  descending order you always drop down  that first number you multiply on the  diagonal and you add straight down you  multiply on the diagonal and you add  straight down multiply on the diagonal  add straight down this last one is a  remainder in this case there's zero  remainder which is what we expected  because we knew that X plus 2 is a  factor and when you do the synthetic  division you go down by one degree so  you go from X cubed down to x squared  and descending order now what we can do  is we can factor this further we can say  what two numbers multiply to negative 4  would add to positive 3 that's going to  be positive 4 and negative 1 to see for  X and negative 1 X adds up to 3x 4 times  negative 1 is negative 4 and we get our  x squared back so these are our  remaining factors X plus 4 and X minus 1  if you want to write the complete linear  factorization we can then add on X plus  2 here so if you were to multiply all  this together we'd get back that  original polynomial if you wanted to  graph it now  by setting all these factors equal to 0  you get the zeroes or the x-intercepts  so we know it's going to cross that  negative 2 negative 4 and positive 1 we  know the leading coefficient is positive  so it's going to go up to the right we  know it's an odd degree it's 3 so it's  going to go the opposite way to the left  all these zeros occurred only once and  you can get a good sketch of your  polynomial function now if you want to  learn more about how to graph polynomial  functions and using the rational root  theorem and synthetic division and kind  of like put it all together you can  follow me over that video right there  where I dive into more examples like  these I'll see you in that video
 

